<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> rent skis until you ski more than about 7–10 days a season or have settled on a style of skiing. Buy boots first: boots that fit your feet make the biggest difference, and they're the part of rental gear that varies most in comfort.</p></div>

<h2>A simple way to decide</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>1–5 days a year:</strong> rent everything. Rentals are maintained, tuned, and swapped easily if something doesn't fit.</li>
  <li><strong>5–10 days a year:</strong> buy boots, keep renting skis. You get comfort and consistency where it matters most, without hauling skis.</li>
  <li><strong>10+ days a year, or a home mountain:</strong> buying skis usually pays off over a few seasons, especially if you drive rather than fly.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why boots come first</h2>
<p>Ski boots control the skis and decide whether your feet are comfortable or in pain by lunchtime. Rental boots are built to fit many feet reasonably, not yours precisely. A boot fitter at a ski shop will measure your feet, check flex and width, and adjust the shell and liner. Expect a snug fit: your toes should lightly touch the front when standing straight, and pull back when you flex forward.</p>

<h2>When renting skis is the better call</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>You fly to ski.</strong> Airline ski bag fees and hauling a bag through airports add up.</li>
  <li><strong>Conditions vary.</strong> Rental "demo" skis let you choose wide skis for a powder week or narrower carving skis for groomers.</li>
  <li><strong>You're still progressing.</strong> The right ski changes as you go from greens to blues to off-piste.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to rent smart</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Reserve online before busy weekends and holidays; it's often cheaper and faster.</li>
  <li>Shops in town are often cheaper than slopeside, and many let you pick up the evening before.</li>
  <li>Tell the tech your real height, weight, and ability. These set the binding release, which is a safety setting.</li>
  <li>Ask to swap boots if they hurt after a run or two. Shops expect it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Buying used</h2>
<p>End-of-season sales and ski swaps are good places to find skis. Avoid used boots unless they've been fitted to you, and have any used skis' bindings inspected and adjusted by a shop.</p>
